is something else that I must tell you." He admitted to me that he suspected Lucy and

45
00:04:57.321 --> 00:05:03.574
the children were the victims of vampires. At first, I just laughed at him; he begged me

46
00:05:03.575 --> 00:05:11.582
to follow him out to the cemetery so he could provide the evidence. I did as he asked,

47
00:05:11.701 --> 00:05:18.502
and he led me to the mausoleum where Lucy’s coffin was held. Once inside, we lifted off

48
00:05:18.503 --> 00:05:27.533
the cover of her casket and─my word!─Lucy’s body was missing! I knew that we had to inform Arthur,

49
00:05:27.534 --> 00:05:38.612
so the next morning, I sent a letter to him, asking that he meet us in front

50
00:05:38.613 --> 00:05:46.398
of the cemetery after tea time. "What are we doing here?" he asked as we entered the

51
00:05:46.399 --> 00:05:51.628
mausoleum, but we offered no explanation. Instead, we walked over to the coffin and began to lift off

52
00:05:51.629 --> 00:05:56.645
the lid. "No!" Arthur yelled, but his pleas went unheeded. There, lying in the casket was the

53
00:05:56.646 --> 00:06:03.574
body of Lucy, still looking like the day she died. Against Arthur’s protests, Van Helsing reached down

54
00:06:03.575 --> 00:06:15.764
and pulled back her lip, revealing sharp, white teeth, while I examined her clothes and saw that her

55
00:06:15.765 --> 00:06:23.688
skirt was caked with mud. I pointed this out to Arthur, who wailed, "This makes no sense!

56
00:06:23.689 --> 00:06:31.810
What has happened to my poor Lucy?" "We have a lot to discuss, my boy," said Van Helsing,

57
00:06:31.811 --> 00:06:40.836
and he told him of his suspicions. Due to either denial or naiveté, Arthur was not convinced

58
00:06:40.837 --> 00:06:49.050
of the professor’s explanation and needed more proof. At midnight, we returned to the mausoleum together, but

59
00:06:49.051 --> 00:06:56.984
this time, Lucy was not there when we arrived. "We must watch for her return," Van Helsing cautioned,

60
00:06:56.985 --> 00:07:07.238
pulling us out into the starry night to wait. From the corner of his eye, Arthur spotted

61
00:07:07.239 --> 00:07:14.167
something white moving through the woods and jerked forward, calling out, "Lucy!" It was she! The professor pulled

62
00:07:14.168 --> 00:07:21.968
Arthur backward, but not before he had attracted Lucy’s attention. She came closer and we could see

63
00:07:21.969 --> 00:07:24.936
that she was dragging something behind her─a child! She was the one attacking children! Lucy dropped the

64
00:07:24.937 --> 00:07:30.993
child a few feet away and then walked over to Arthur. "Hello, my love," she taunted him. "Give

65
00:07:30.994 --> 00:07:37.414
me a kiss . . ." When she leaned toward us, I could smell the reek of

66
00:07:37.415 --> 00:07:43.461
death on her breath. Arthur thrust a gold cross at her, and she screamed, fell backward, and

67
00:07:43.462 --> 00:07:50.731
then turned to run away. After several moments, he spoke: "I know what we must do. And we

68
00:07:50.732 --> 00:07:55.978
must do it soon, before she harms anyone else." There was plenty of work to be done

69
00:07:55.979 --> 00:08:01.144
before we could confront Lucy again, and with this in mind, we returned to the house to begin

70
00:08:01.145 --> 00:08:11.001
preparations. Van Helsing carved a sharp, pointed stake out of wood, and I asked the servants to

71
00:08:11.244 --> 00:08:23.701
prepare for us three garlic necklaces, which the professor believed would give us extra protection. When it

72
00:08:23.702 --> 00:08:29.146
was light again, we collected our tools and ventured out to the cemetery. In the mausoleum, we left

73
00:08:29.147 --> 00:08:35.888
Arthur alone to grieve and say a final good-bye to his fiancée. We didn’t have to wait

74
00:08:35.889 --> 00:08:46.322
very long, because before much time had passed, he emerged with a determined look on his face and

75
